<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scupi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Scupi was an ancient city on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardar">Vardar</a>, just north-west of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>. Originally a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dardanians">Dardani</a>, it became a Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> base and then, under the Flavian emperors, a colony of veterans, later serving as capital of the province of Dardania. An earthquake destroyed it in 518, and its ruins have been excavated since the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scupi.jpg" alt="Scupi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dimitar Georgievski · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skopje_and_Skupi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The site lies between the hill of Zajčev Rid and the Vardar River, several kilometres from the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a>. According to the archaeologist Ivan Mikulčić, Scupi was once twice the size of Stobi, five times larger than Heraclea near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitola">Bitola</a> and nine times larger than Bargala near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stip">Štip</a>, the largest known Roman settlement in the region. Archaeologists distinguish four building periods, from a military camp of the second century BCE to the early Christian town that ended in 518.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dardanians">Dardanians</a> had stayed independent after the Roman conquest of Macedonia because they had supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, and they most likely lost their independence in 28 BCE. Scupi grew as a colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionaries</a>, mainly veterans of the Legio VII Claudia, in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a>, and became the chief centre from which Dardania was romanised. It belonged to Moesia Superior after that province was formed and from 272 to the province of Dardania. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> ravaged it in 269 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> in the 5th century.</p><p>Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> is thought to date from the 2nd century, as the seats bear the name Colonia Scupi Aelia, and may have been built for a visit of the emperor Hadrian. After the earthquake of 518 life in Scupi stopped; its people are assumed to have moved to Kale, a hill in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a>, and much of the theatre's stone was probably carried there. Some scholars connect this move with Justiniana Prima, the city <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> founded near his birthplace, though the identification rests on assumptions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Excavation began between the two World Wars, when Radoslav Gruić found the early Christian basilica in 1925 and Nikola Vulić the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>. The Museum of the City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a> has worked on the site since 1966, and in July 2008 a statue of Venus 1.7 metres tall was found there. In 2016 Scupi was designated a cultural heritage site of exceptional significance; a restoration project begun that year was halted in 2017.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Scupi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Dardani lived in the plains of Kosovo and around <b>Scupi</b> (Skopje) from at least the 4th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/dardanian-kingdom">Dardanian Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
